        "math_academy_id": "q-123470",
        "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e8\\frac{2}{t},\\,2t\u27e9$ and $F(1)=\u27e81,\\,3\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e8\\frac{2}{t},\\,2t\u27e9\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8\\frac{2}{t},\\,2t\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|t|+{c}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({t}^{2})+{c}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=\u27e81,\\,3\u27e9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({1}^{2})+{c}_{1},\\,(1)^{2}+{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e81,\\,3\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8{c}_{1},\\,1+{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e81,\\,3\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({t}^{2})+1,\\,{t}^{2}+2\u27e9.\n$$",
